//? Title: Linear Mixed Models: Partial Pooling for Grouped Data
//? Subtitle: Random intercepts and slopes, profiled ML and REML, and BLUP shrinkage derived, checked by hand on a balanced one-way design, tested by simulation, and applied to children's orthodontic growth, using datamunge's stats::LMM
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Grouped data, such as pupils in schools or repeated measurements on the same child, break the
independence that ordinary regression assumes. Pooling every row into one least-squares fit
gets the standard errors wrong, and fitting each group separately gives noisy estimates for
small groups. A linear mixed model treats the group effects as random draws with an estimated
variance, which decides how far each group's estimate is pulled towards the population: partial
pooling. We derive the model's marginal covariance, generalised least squares, the profiled ML
and REML criteria that datamunge's `stats::LMM` minimises, and the best linear unbiased
predictors (BLUPs). On a balanced one-way design of twelve numbers, the library reproduces the
ANOVA closed forms for both ML and REML to six decimals, and the BLUPs equal the group means
shrunk by the reliability \(n\tau^2/(n\tau^2+\sigma^2)\), exactly, for unequal group sizes too.
Simulations confirm the theory: REML removes ML's downward bias in the group variance; the
estimates that land on zero are exactly the data sets the \(F\) statistic predicts; OLS reports a
standard error 2.4 times too small for a group-level covariate (95% intervals cover 59% of the
time); BLUPs beat group means for every group size; and the likelihood-ratio test for a zero
variance is far more conservative than \(\chi^2_1\) or even the 50:50 boundary mixture suggest. On
children's orthodontic growth the fit agrees with R's nlme to seven digits; boys grow
0.30 mm/year faster than girls (\(p=0.024\)), and OLS misstates every standard error, by up to a
factor of 1.7.
)

\toc

> The problem

Many data sets come in groups: pupils in schools, repeated measurements on the same patient,
plots in the same field. Observations in one group share influences that the covariates do not
record, so they are more alike than observations from different groups. Two simple analyses
both go wrong. Ordinary least squares on all the rows (*complete pooling*) treats the
observations as independent. Its estimates are still unbiased, but its standard errors are too
small for anything that varies between groups, because \(n\) correlated rows carry less
information than \(n\) independent ones. Fitting each group separately (*no pooling*) respects the
grouping, but a group with two observations gets an estimate as noisy as two observations allow,
and nothing can be said about a group that was not sampled.

A linear mixed model treats the group effects as draws from a population with a variance that
is estimated from the data. The variance decides how far each group's estimate is pulled
towards the overall mean: a lot when groups barely differ or the group is small, hardly at all
when groups differ a lot or the group is large. This is *partial pooling*.

> The method

>> The model

Observations \(y_{gi}\), \(i=1,\dots,n_g\), in groups \(g=1,\dots,G\), with \(n=\sum_gn_g\). Each row has
fixed-effect covariates \(x_{gi}\in\mathbb R^p\) and random-effect covariates \(z_{gi}\in\mathbb R^q\) (for a
random intercept \(z_{gi}=1\); for a random intercept and slope in age, \(z_{gi}=(1,\text{age}_{gi})\)):

$$
y_g=X_g\beta+Z_gb_g+\varepsilon_g,\qquad b_g\sim N(0,\Sigma),\qquad \varepsilon_g\sim N(0,\sigma^2I_{n_g}),
$$
\alttext(The response vector of group g equals X g times beta plus Z g times b g plus epsilon g, where the random effect b g is normal with mean 0 and covariance Sigma, and the error epsilon g is normal with mean 0 and covariance sigma squared times the identity.)

independently across groups. \(\beta\) is the population (fixed) effect, the same for everyone;
\(b_g\) is group \(g\)'s deviation from it. In the formula language this is
`y ~ x + (1 + age | subject)`: the terms left of `|` are the columns of \(Z\), and the
variable on the right defines the groups.

Integrating out \(b_g\) gives the *marginal* model. The rows of one group are jointly normal
with

$$
y_g\sim N\big(X_g\beta,\ V_g\big),\qquad V_g=\sigma^2I+Z_g\Sigma Z_g^\top .
$$
\alttext(y g is normal with mean X g beta and covariance V g, where V g equals sigma squared times the identity plus Z g Sigma Z g transpose.)

For a random intercept alone, \(\Sigma=\tau^2\) and \(V_g=\sigma^2I+\tau^2\mathbf 1\mathbf 1^\top\): every pair of rows in a
group has correlation \(\rho=\tau^2/(\tau^2+\sigma^2)\), the *intraclass correlation*, and rows in different
groups are independent.

>> Generalised least squares, given the variances

If \(V\) (block diagonal, blocks \(V_g\)) were known, the best linear unbiased estimator of \(\beta\)
would be generalised least squares,

$$
\hat\beta=(X^\top V^{-1}X)^{-1}X^\top V^{-1}y,\qquad \operatorname{Var}(\hat\beta)=(X^\top V^{-1}X)^{-1}.
$$
\alttext(beta hat equals the inverse of X transpose V inverse X, times X transpose V inverse y. Its variance is the inverse of X transpose V inverse X.)

OLS is the special case \(V=\sigma^2I\). With correlated rows, OLS is still unbiased but no longer
efficient, and its usual variance formula is wrong. For a covariate \(w\) that is constant within
groups, with \(m\) rows per group and a random intercept, the OLS formula understates the variance
of \(\hat\beta_w\) by the *design effect* \(1+(m-1)\rho\): the \(m\) rows of one group carry roughly one
group's worth of information about \(w\).

>> Estimating the variances: profiled maximum likelihood

Write \(\Sigma=\sigma^2\Lambda\Lambda^\top\) with \(\Lambda\) lower triangular, and collect its \(q(q+1)/2\) entries into
\(\theta\) (row by row). \(\theta\) is the random-effect covariance relative to the residual variance; for
a random intercept, \(\theta=\tau/\sigma\). Then \(V_g=\sigma^2M_g\) with \(M_g=I+Z_g\Lambda\Lambda^\top Z_g^\top\), which depends
on \(\theta\) only. The log-likelihood of the marginal model is

$$
-2\ell(\beta,\sigma^2,\theta)=n\log(2\pi\sigma^2)+\sum_g\log|M_g|+\frac{1}{\sigma^2}\sum_g(y_g-X_g\beta)^\top M_g^{-1}(y_g-X_g\beta).
$$
\alttext(Minus 2 times the log-likelihood equals n log of 2 pi sigma squared, plus the sum over groups of log determinant M g, plus one over sigma squared times the sum over groups of the quadratic form of the residual y g minus X g beta in M g inverse.)

For fixed \(\theta\), the minimiser over \(\beta\) is GLS with \(M\) in place of \(V\) (the factor
\(\sigma^2\) cancels), and the minimiser over \(\sigma^2\) is \(\hat\sigma^2_\theta=r^\top M^{-1}r/n\) with
\(r=y-X\hat\beta_\theta\). Substituting both leaves a function of \(\theta\) alone, the *profiled deviance*

$$
d_{\mathrm{ML}}(\theta)=n\log(2\pi\hat\sigma^2_\theta)+n+\sum_g\log|M_g(\theta)|,
$$
\alttext(The profiled ML deviance of theta equals n log of 2 pi sigma hat squared of theta, plus n, plus the sum over groups of log determinant M g of theta.)

which has only \(q(q+1)/2\) arguments: one for a random intercept, three for an intercept and
slope. `LMM::deviance_at(theta)` evaluates it.

>> REML

The ML estimate of \(\sigma^2\) divides by \(n\), ignoring that \(p\) degrees of freedom were spent on
\(\hat\beta\), just as \(\frac1n\sum(y_i-\bar y)^2\) underestimates a variance. The variance components inherit
the same bias, and with few groups it is large. *Restricted* maximum likelihood (REML) maximises
the likelihood of \(n-p\) error contrasts \(K^\top y\) with \(K^\top X=0\), which do not depend on \(\beta\).
Up to a constant, its profiled criterion is

$$
d_{\mathrm{REML}}(\theta)=(n-p)\log(2\pi\hat\sigma^2_\theta)+(n-p)+\sum_g\log|M_g|+\log|X^\top M^{-1}X|,
\qquad \hat\sigma^2_\theta=\frac{r^\top M^{-1}r}{n-p}.
$$
\alttext(The profiled REML criterion equals n minus p times log of 2 pi sigma hat squared, plus n minus p, plus the sum of log determinants of M g, plus the log determinant of X transpose M inverse X, where sigma hat squared equals r transpose M inverse r divided by n minus p.)

The extra term \(\log|X^\top M^{-1}X|\) is the penalty for estimating \(\beta\). REML is the default
(`LMMOptions::reml`, as in lme4 and nlme). Two REML fits can be compared by their criteria only
when they have the same fixed effects; to compare fixed effects, fit by ML.

>> The balanced one-way model in closed form

With a random intercept only, \(G\) groups of equal size \(m\) and no covariates, everything is
explicit in terms of the one-way ANOVA mean squares

$$
\text{MSB}=\frac{m\sum_g(\bar y_g-\bar y)^2}{G-1},\qquad \text{MSW}=\frac{\sum_g\sum_i(y_{gi}-\bar y_g)^2}{G(m-1)} .
$$
\alttext(The between-group mean square MSB equals m times the sum over groups of the squared difference between the group mean and the grand mean, divided by G minus 1. The within-group mean square MSW equals the sum of squared deviations from the group means, divided by G times m minus 1.)

Since \(E[\text{MSW}]=\sigma^2\) and \(E[\text{MSB}]=\sigma^2+m\tau^2\), the estimates are (Searle, Casella and McCulloch,
1992, chapter 3)

$$
\hat\beta_0=\bar y,\qquad \hat\sigma^2=\text{MSW},\qquad
\hat\tau^2_{\mathrm{REML}}=\frac{\text{MSB}-\text{MSW}}{m},\qquad
\hat\tau^2_{\mathrm{ML}}=\frac{\tfrac{G-1}{G}\text{MSB}-\text{MSW}}{m},
$$
\alttext(beta 0 hat equals the grand mean, sigma hat squared equals MSW, the REML estimate of tau squared equals MSB minus MSW over m, and the ML estimate of tau squared equals G minus 1 over G times MSB, minus MSW, all over m.)

when these are positive, and \(\hat\tau^2=0\) otherwise. The REML estimate is the unbiased ANOVA
estimator. ML shrinks MSB by \((G-1)/G\), which is a large bias with few groups. The standard error
of the intercept is \(\sqrt{\text{MSB}/(mG)}\) under REML: the variance of a mean of \(G\) independent group
means, each with variance \(\tau^2+\sigma^2/m\).

Under \(\tau^2=0\), \(F=\text{MSB}/\text{MSW}\) has an \(F_{G-1,\,G(m-1)}\) distribution, and in general
\(F/(1+m\tau^2/\sigma^2)\) does. So the probability that the estimate lands on the boundary is known exactly:

$$
P(\hat\tau^2_{\mathrm{REML}}=0)=F_{G-1,G(m-1)}\Big(\frac{1}{1+m\tau^2/\sigma^2}\Big),\qquad
P(\hat\tau^2_{\mathrm{ML}}=0)=F_{G-1,G(m-1)}\Big(\frac{G/(G-1)}{1+m\tau^2/\sigma^2}\Big).
$$
\alttext(The probability that the REML estimate of tau squared is 0 equals the F cumulative distribution function with G minus 1 and G times m minus 1 degrees of freedom, evaluated at 1 over 1 plus m tau squared over sigma squared. For ML the argument is G over G minus 1, divided by the same quantity.)

>> Predicting the group effects: BLUPs

The random effects are not parameters, but given the data they have a conditional
distribution. Its mean, with the estimates plugged in, is the *best linear unbiased predictor*
(Robinson, 1991)

$$
\hat b_g=\Sigma Z_g^\top V_g^{-1}(y_g-X_g\hat\beta)=\Lambda\Lambda^\top Z_g^\top M_g^{-1}r_g .
$$
\alttext(b g hat equals Sigma Z g transpose V g inverse times the residual y g minus X g beta hat, which equals Lambda Lambda transpose Z g transpose M g inverse r g.)

For a random intercept, \(\mathbf 1^\top V_g^{-1}=\mathbf 1^\top/(\sigma^2+n_g\tau^2)\), so

$$
\hat b_g=\frac{n_g\tau^2}{n_g\tau^2+\sigma^2}\,\big(\bar y_g-\bar x_g^\top\hat\beta\big).
$$
\alttext(b g hat equals n g tau squared over n g tau squared plus sigma squared, times the group's mean residual.)

The group's own mean residual is multiplied by a *shrinkage factor* between 0 and 1: the
reliability of a mean of \(n_g\) observations. A large or very distinct group keeps nearly all of
its own estimate; a small group in a population of similar groups is pulled to the overall
line. With known variances, this weighted average of no pooling (factor 1) and complete pooling
(factor 0) has the smallest mean squared prediction error of any linear predictor. With the
variances estimated from the data it is an empirical Bayes estimator, in the same family as
the James–Stein estimator.

>> Computation

`stats::LMM` parses the formula, builds \(X\) (dummy columns for factors, products for
interactions) and \(Z\), and minimises the profiled criterion over \(\theta\). A global search
(differential evolution, seeded, `de_population_size` × `de_max_generations`) covers a box
with the diagonal entries of \(\Lambda\) in \([0,b]\) and the others in \([-b,b]\), where \(b\) is
`theta_bound` (default 5; raise it if a group SD may exceed five residual SDs). Nelder–Mead
then polishes the best point, and the signs of \(\Lambda\)'s columns are fixed so that its diagonal is
non-negative. Each evaluation needs one Cholesky factorisation of each
\(n_g\times n_g\) block \(M_g\), so the cost is linear in the number of groups. At the optimum,
\(\hat\beta\), its covariance \(\hat\sigma^2(X^\top M^{-1}X)^{-1}\), Wald \(z\)-statistics and the BLUPs are computed
once. `coefficient_frame()` and `random_effects_frame()` return them as data frames.

> Simple examples

>> A balanced one-way design by hand

Four groups of three:

| group | values | mean |
| :--- | :--- | ---: |
| A | 3, 3, 6 | 4 |
| B | 3, 6, 6 | 5 |
| C | 6, 7, 8 | 7 |
| D | 9, 9, 6 | 8 |

The grand mean is 6. The deviations of the group means are \((-2,-1,1,2)\), so
\(\text{MSB}=3\cdot10/3=10\). The within-group sums of squares are \(6,6,2,6\), so \(\text{MSW}=20/8=2.5\) and
\(F=4\). REML gives \(\hat\sigma^2=2.5\), \(\hat\tau^2=(10-2.5)/3=2.5\), so \(\hat\theta=\hat\tau/\hat\sigma=1\) and \(\rho=0.5\). The
shrinkage factor is \(3\cdot2.5/(3\cdot2.5+2.5)=0.75\), so the BLUPs are \(0.75\cdot(-2,-1,1,2)=(-1.5,-0.75,0.75,1.5)\),
and the intercept has standard error \(\sqrt{10/12}=0.913\). ML gives
\(\hat\tau^2=(\tfrac34\cdot10-2.5)/3=\tfrac53\), \(\hat\theta=\sqrt{2/3}=0.816\), shrinkage factor
\(5/7.5=\tfrac23\) and BLUPs \((-\tfrac43,-\tfrac23,\tfrac23,\tfrac43)\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lmm_oneway.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

using DF = dm::dstruct::DataFrame;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::string> names{"A", "B", "C", "D"};
  const std::vector<std::vector<double>> values{{3, 3, 6}, {3, 6, 6}, {6, 7, 8}, {9, 9, 6}};
  std::vector<std::string> group;
  std::vector<double> y;
  for (std::size_t g = 0; g < 4; ++g)
    for (const double v : values[g]) group.push_back(names[g]), y.push_back(v);
  DF data;
  data.add_column("group", group);
  data.add_column("y", y);

  const dm::stats::LMM reml(data, "y ~ 1 + (1 | group)");
  const dm::stats::LMM ml(data, "y ~ 1 + (1 | group)", {.reml = false});
  DF table;
  table.add_column("quantity", std::vector<std::string>{"beta0", "se(beta0)", "sigma^2", "tau^2", "theta",
                                                        "BLUP A", "BLUP B", "BLUP C", "BLUP D"});
  auto column = [](const dm::stats::LMM& fit) {
    const double tau2 = fit.random_effect_covariance()(0, 0);
    std::vector<double> c{fit.coefficients()[0], fit.standard_errors()[0], fit.residual_variance(), tau2,
                          fit.theta()[0]};
    for (const auto& b : fit.random_effects()) c.push_back(b[0]);
    return c;
  };
  table.add_column("REML", column(reml));
  table.add_column("REML by hand", std::vector<double>{6, std::sqrt(10.0 / 12), 2.5, 2.5, 1, -1.5, -0.75, 0.75, 1.5});
  table.add_column("ML", column(ml));
  table.add_column("ML by hand", std::vector<double>{6, std::sqrt(7.5 / 12), 2.5, 5.0 / 3, std::sqrt(2.0 / 3),
                                                     -4.0 / 3, -2.0 / 3, 2.0 / 3, 4.0 / 3});
  DF::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 6;
  std::print("{}\n\n", table.to_md(md));
  std::print("REML criterion {:.4f}, ML deviance {:.4f}\n\n", reml.deviance(), ml.deviance());

  // Left: the data and the three kinds of pooling.
  auto pool = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  pool.abline_h(6.0, dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 2.0);
  for (std::size_t g = 0; g < 4; ++g) {
    const double x = g + 1.0, mean = (values[g][0] + values[g][1] + values[g][2]) / 3.0;
    pool.points({x - 0.08, x, x + 0.08}, values[g], dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 6.0);
    pool.segments({x - 0.3}, {mean}, {x + 0.3}, {mean}, dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 3.0);
    pool.segments({x - 0.3}, {6.0 + reml.random_effects()[g][0]}, {x + 0.3}, {6.0 + reml.random_effects()[g][0]},
                  dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 3.0);
    pool.text(x, 2.2, names[g], dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 14.0);
  }
  pool.legend({"complete pooling", "no pooling", "partial (REML)"},
              {dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}});
  pool.x_limits(0.4, 4.6).y_limits(1.5, 12.5).title("Group means and BLUPs").x_label("group").y_label("y");

  // Right: the profiled criteria against theta = tau / sigma.
  auto prof = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> th, dr, dml;
  for (int i = 0; i <= 200; ++i) {
    const double t = 2.5 * i / 200.0;
    th.push_back(t);
    dr.push_back(reml.deviance_at({t}) - reml.deviance());
    dml.push_back(ml.deviance_at({t}) - ml.deviance());
  }
  prof.lines(th, dr, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}).lines(th, dml, dm::RGB{37, 99, 235});
  prof.text(2.2, 1.5, "REML", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 14.0).text(1.75, 3.0, "ML", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 14.0);
  prof.abline_v(reml.theta()[0], dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1.0).abline_v(ml.theta()[0], dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 1.0);
  std::print("Criterion at theta = 0 above its minimum: REML {:.3f}, ML {:.3f}\n", dr[0], dml[0]);
  std::print("Largest plotted value: {:.3f}\n\n", std::max(std::ranges::max(dr), std::ranges::max(dml)));
  prof.x_limits(0.0, 2.5).y_limits(0.0, 4.0).title("Profiled criteria").x_label("theta = tau / sigma")
      .y_label("criterion - minimum");

  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(pool).add(prof).size(1100, 480).fit_panels();
  fig.save_svg("build/lmm_oneway.svg");
}
```
