// result_end

\caption(Left: the twelve observations (black), the complete-pooling estimate \(\bar y=6\) (grey), the group means (blue) and the REML partial-pooling estimates \(\hat\beta_0+\hat b_g\) (red), pulled a quarter of the way to 6. Right: the profiled REML and ML criteria against \(\theta=\tau/\sigma\), minus their minima; vertical lines mark the estimates. Table: the library's estimates against the closed forms.)
\alttext(Left: four groups A to D on the x axis with three black points each. A grey horizontal line at 6. Short blue bars at the group means 4, 5, 7 and 8, and short red bars at 4.5, 5.25, 6.75 and 7.5, each closer to 6. Right: two U-shaped curves of criterion minus minimum against theta from 0 to 3, the red REML curve with its minimum at theta equals 1 and the blue ML curve with its minimum at about 0.82.)

Both fits reproduce every closed form to six decimals. In the left panel, the partial-pooling
estimates (red) sit a quarter of the way from the group means (blue) to the grand mean (grey);
under ML they would sit a third of the way, because ML's smaller \(\hat\tau^2\) gives a smaller
reliability. The right panel shows the two profiled criteria. Each has a single minimum, at
\(\theta=1\) for REML and \(\theta=0.816\) for ML; ML's minimum is to the left because it ignores the
degree of freedom spent on \(\hat\beta_0\). At \(\theta=0\) (no group effect) the criteria are 2.42 and
1.73 above their minima: the data favour a group effect, but with four groups not decisively.

>> Unequal group sizes: shrinkage depends on n

With unequal group sizes there is no closed form for the variances, but given them the BLUP
is still the group's mean residual times \(n_g\hat\tau^2/(n_g\hat\tau^2+\hat\sigma^2)\). Six groups of sizes 1 to 32
are simulated with \(\tau=1\), \(\sigma=2\) and mean 5.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

using DF = dm::dstruct::DataFrame;

int main() {
  dm::random::Xoshiro256StarStar rng(3);
  const std::vector<int> sizes{1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32};
  std::vector<std::string> group;
  std::vector<double> y;
  for (std::size_t g = 0; g < sizes.size(); ++g) {
    const double b = rng.normal(0.0, 1.0);
    for (int i = 0; i < sizes[g]; ++i) group.push_back(std::format("g{}", g + 1)), y.push_back(5.0 + b + rng.normal(0.0, 2.0));
  }
  DF data;
  data.add_column("group", group);
  data.add_column("y", y);
  const dm::stats::LMM fit(data, "y ~ 1 + (1 | group)");
  const double tau2 = fit.random_effect_covariance()(0, 0), sigma2 = fit.residual_variance();
  std::print("beta0 = {:.4f}, tau^2 = {:.4f}, sigma^2 = {:.4f}\n\n", fit.coefficients()[0], tau2, sigma2);

  auto frame = fit.random_effects_frame();
  std::vector<double> mean_resid, factor, ratio;
  for (std::size_t g = 0; g < sizes.size(); ++g) {
    double s = 0.0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < y.size(); ++i) if (group[i] == fit.group_labels()[g]) s += y[i];
    const double n = frame.get_column<double>("n").values()[g];
    mean_resid.push_back(s / n - fit.coefficients()[0]);
    factor.push_back(n * tau2 / (n * tau2 + sigma2));
    ratio.push_back(fit.random_effects()[g][0] / mean_resid.back());
  }
  frame.add_column("mean - beta0", mean_resid);
  frame.add_column("BLUP / (mean - beta0)", ratio);
  frame.add_column("n tau^2 / (n tau^2 + sigma^2)", factor);
  DF::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 4;
  std::print("{}\n", frame.to_md(md));
}
```
