\image(figures/lmm_orthodont.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Distance against age for the girls (left) and boys (right). Grey points are the measurements, thin grey lines each child's fitted line \(\hat\beta+\hat b_g\) from the random intercept and slope model, and the thick line the population line for that sex. Tables: the fixed effects with their mixed-model and OLS standard errors; the variance components of three models; the first four rows of the BLUPs.)
\alttext(Two panels of distance in millimetres against age from 8 to 14. Left, girls: grey points between 16.5 and 28, eleven thin grey nearly parallel lines rising gently, and a thick red line from about 21.2 at age 8 to 24.1 at age 14. Right, boys: grey points between 17 and 31.5, sixteen thin grey lines rising more steeply, and a thick blue line from about 22.6 at age 8 to 27.3 at age 14.)

The random intercept and slope model matches nlme's fit of the same model (the regression test
`RandomSlopeMatchesNlmeOnOrthodont` checks it to \(10^{-7}\)). The population lines are
\(22.65+0.480(\text{age}-11)\) mm for girls and \(24.97+0.784(\text{age}-11)\) mm for boys. At age 11 the
boys are 2.32 mm larger (SE 0.76), and they grow 0.30 mm/year faster (SE 0.135, \(p=0.024\)).

*Centring changes the parametrisation, not the model.* With age centred at 11, the intercept SD
is 1.83 mm and the intercept and slope are almost uncorrelated (0.21). With raw age the
intercept is the extrapolated distance at birth, which varies more (SD 2.41) and correlates
\(-0.67\) with the slope, since a steeper line extrapolates to a lower start. The slope SD, the
residual SD and the restricted log-likelihood are identical: \(\Lambda\) has only been re-expressed in
another basis.

*Is a random slope needed?* The children's growth rates vary with SD 0.18 mm/year, but the
REML likelihood-ratio statistic against the intercept-only model is 1.18. Adding a random slope
puts its variance on the boundary and adds a free covariance, so the reference distribution is
the 50:50 mixture of \(\chi^2_1\) and \(\chi^2_2\) (Stram and Lee, 1994): \(p=0.42\). The thin lines in the figure are
indeed close to parallel. Comparing the two REML fits is valid because their fixed effects are
the same.

*OLS gets the estimates right and the uncertainty wrong.* Every child is measured at the same
four ages, so GLS and OLS give identical fixed effects. Their standard errors differ in both
directions. For the between-child contrasts (the intercept and the sex difference), OLS
treats 108 rows as independent when there are only 27 children, and its standard errors are
too small by a factor of 1.7. For the within-child effects (age and the age-by-sex
interaction), OLS leaves the children's level differences in the residual, and its standard
errors are 1.5 times too large.

> Conclusion

A linear mixed model accounts for grouping by giving the group effects a distribution and
estimating its covariance from the data. Profiling out \(\beta\) and \(\sigma^2\) reduces the fit to a
minimisation over the relative covariance factor \(\theta\), one number for a random intercept and
three for an intercept and slope. REML corrects ML's downward bias in the variances, which is
large with few groups. Both were checked here against the closed forms of the balanced one-way
design, and the random-slope model against nlme on the Orthodont data, to machine or optimiser
precision. The BLUPs shrink each group's own estimate by its reliability \(n\tau^2/(n\tau^2+\sigma^2)\), and
in simulation they beat both no pooling and complete pooling for every group size. The
simulations also show where the method's standard inference is weak. Wald intervals for
group-level covariates are a little too narrow with a dozen groups, and likelihood-ratio tests
of a variance component are conservative even against the boundary mixture. Ignoring the
grouping is worse: OLS's intervals for a group-level covariate covered 59% of the time, and on
the Orthodont data its standard errors were off by a factor of 1.5 to 1.7 in both directions.

> References

